{%- comment -%}
  Wishlist drawer — rendered ONCE from layout/theme.liquid, on every page.

  Deliberately mirrors Dawn's cart drawer contract rather than inventing a second drawer
  system: a custom element that toggles `.active`, a sibling overlay that closes on click,
  Escape to close, `document.body.classList.add('overflow-hidden')` for scroll lock, and
  Dawn's own `trapFocus`/`removeTrapFocus` from global.js. Behaviour lives in
  assets/aria-wishlist.js.

  It does NOT reuse the `.drawer` CSS classes, on purpose: `component-cart-drawer.css` is
  only loaded when `settings.cart_type == 'drawer'`, so borrowing those class names would
  make the wishlist silently lose all its styling the day a merchant switches the cart to
  a notification/page. The wishlist ships its own stylesheet instead.

  The item list is empty in the markup and filled by JS from localStorage. There is no
  server-side record of what a visitor has wishlisted, so Liquid has nothing to render.
{%- endcomment -%}
<aria-wishlist-drawer
  class="aria-wl-drawer"
  data-wishlist-drawer
  role="dialog"
  aria-modal="true"
  aria-labelledby="AriaWishlistDrawer-Heading"
  aria-hidden="true"
>
  <div class="aria-wl-drawer__overlay" data-wishlist-overlay></div>

  <div class="aria-wl-drawer__inner">
    <header class="aria-wl-drawer__header">
      <h2 class="aria-wl-drawer__heading" id="AriaWishlistDrawer-Heading">
        {{ 'aria_theme.wishlist.title' | t }}
        {%- render 'aria-icon', name: 'heart', class: 'aria-wl-drawer__heading-icon' -%}
      </h2>

      <button
        type="button"
        class="aria-wl-drawer__close"
        data-wishlist-close
        aria-label="{{ 'aria_theme.wishlist.close' | t }}"
      >
        {%- render 'aria-icon', name: 'close' -%}
      </button>
    </header>

    {%- comment -%}
      Both states live in the markup and JS toggles `hidden` between them, so switching to
      and from the empty state never rebuilds the drawer chrome.
    {%- endcomment -%}
    <div class="aria-wl-drawer__body" data-wishlist-body>
      <p class="aria-wl-drawer__empty" data-wishlist-empty>
        <span class="aria-wl-drawer__empty-title">{{ 'aria_theme.wishlist.empty_title' | t }}</span>
        <span class="aria-wl-drawer__empty-text">{{ 'aria_theme.wishlist.empty_text' | t }}</span>
      </p>

      <ul class="aria-wl-drawer__items" data-wishlist-items role="list" hidden></ul>
    </div>

    <footer class="aria-wl-drawer__footer">
      <button type="button" class="aria-wl-drawer__continue" data-wishlist-close>
        {{ 'aria_theme.wishlist.continue' | t }}
      </button>
    </footer>
  </div>
</aria-wishlist-drawer>

{%- comment -%}
  Row template. Kept as a <template> rather than built with innerHTML strings in JS so the
  markup stays reviewable next to the rest of the theme, and so every translated string is
  resolved by Liquid instead of being hardcoded in a script.
{%- endcomment -%}
<template id="AriaWishlistItemTemplate">
  <li class="aria-wl-item" data-wishlist-item>
    <a class="aria-wl-item__media" data-wl-link tabindex="-1" aria-hidden="true">
      <img class="aria-wl-item__image" data-wl-image alt="" loading="lazy" width="72" height="72">
    </a>

    <div class="aria-wl-item__details">
      <span class="aria-wl-item__vendor" data-wl-vendor hidden></span>
      <a class="aria-wl-item__title" data-wl-link data-wl-title></a>
      <span class="aria-wl-item__variant" data-wl-variant hidden></span>

      <span class="aria-wl-item__price">
        <span class="aria-wl-item__price-current" data-wl-price></span>
        <s class="aria-wl-item__price-compare" data-wl-compare hidden></s>
      </span>

      <div class="aria-wl-item__actions">
        <button
          type="button"
          class="aria-wl-item__add"
          data-wl-add
          data-sold-out-label="{{ 'aria_theme.wishlist.sold_out' | t | escape }}"
        >
          <span class="aria-wl-item__add-label">{{ 'aria_theme.wishlist.add_to_cart' | t }}</span>
          <span class="aria-wl-item__spinner" aria-hidden="true"></span>
        </button>
        <button type="button" class="aria-wl-item__remove" data-wl-remove>
          {{ 'aria_theme.wishlist.remove' | t }}
        </button>
      </div>

      {%- comment -%}
        Errors are announced politely and shown inline on the row that failed, so a failed
        add never silently leaves the item sitting there looking untouched.
      {%- endcomment -%}
      <p
        class="aria-wl-item__error"
        data-wl-error
        data-fallback="{{ 'aria_theme.wishlist.error' | t | escape }}"
        role="status"
        aria-live="polite"
        hidden
      ></p>
    </div>
  </li>
</template>
